    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "86",
      "title": "Worms at Work: My Visit to a Vermicomposting Unit",
      "description": "",
      "date": "Jul 31, 2025",
      "read_time": "4 min read",
      "author": "Didar Ali",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "86-Banner.png"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "When I first visited the vermicomposting unit at the Aga Khan University's Arusha Climate and Environmental Research Centre (ACER), in Arusha Tanzania, I didn't expect to be so fascinated by worms. But there I was, standing beside rows of composting beds, watching tiny earthworms silently transform piles of discarded leaves and food scraps into dark, crumbly gold. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It didn't smell bad. It didn't look messy. In fact, it felt oddly alive like the soil was breathing, like something powerful was happening beneath the surface. That was the moment I realized how much potential lies in something as humble as worm poop."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "What Is Vermicomposting, anyway?",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Before this visit, I had only a vague idea of what vermicomposting actually meant. Sure, I knew it involved worms and compost. But standing there, listening to one of the staff explain it, everything clicked."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The word comes from vermi, Latin for worm, and composting, which is the process of turning organic waste into fertilizer. Therefore, vermicomposting is the use of worms to recycle organic matter. But it's not just any worms. Specific species of earthworms are used to break down food scraps, manure, crop waste, and other biodegradable material. These worms eat their own body weight in organic matter every day, and what they leave behind is worm castings is packed with nutrients that plants love."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Even more fascinating? The presence of worms boosts microbial activity in the soil, especially actinomycetes, a kind of bacteria that's six times more concentrated in worm castings than in ordinary soil. These microbes are critical for healthy soil and strong plants."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Why Vermicomposting Matters",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "At AKU ACER, vermicomposting isn't just a scientific experiment. It's part of a bigger push toward regenerative agriculture, environmental protection, and community empowerment."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Didier Van Bignoot, an agricultural expert who works with Aga Khan Foundation as a global advisor, explained that vermicompost improves soil structure by increasing air and water flow. This helps roots grow better and makes the soil more resilient, especially in harsh conditions. "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In their paper 'The Importance of Vermicompost in Agricultural Production and Economy\" Kayabaşı and Yılmaz (2021) state that vermicompost is a slow-release fertilizer, which means plants absorb nutrients over time rather than all at once unlike chemical fertilizers that can wash away and pollute water sources."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fredy Wilfred, a community development expert and Value Chain Assistant at at the AKU'S Principal Campus Arusha, "
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "emphasized that vermicomposting also saves money. Farmers who use it often see better yields, spend less on expensive chemicals, and even find their crops become more resistant to disease. Over time, this can make a real difference, especially for small-scale farmers trying to make a living."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "How It Works",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "I got to see the whole process up close. It starts with collecting biodegradable waste: vegetable scraps, crop residues, cow dung, coffee pulps, leaf litter. Basically anything organic. The waste is sorted to remove things like plastic or metal, then it's mixed with cow dung slurry and left for about 20 days to start decomposing. This makes it easier for the worms to digest."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The beds are usually laid on concrete to keep nutrients from seeping into the ground. A layer of soil or sand goes at the bottom, followed by alternating layers of the pre-digested waste and cow dung. Once that's ready, the worms are gently introduced."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Moisture is key. The beds are kept damp (but not soaking) and covered with jute sacks or straw to keep the temperature steady. Too much sun or rain can harm the worms, so the beds are protected with thatched roofs or shades. The worms get to work quietly, and after a few weeks, you're left with rich, black compost. The worms are separated and reused, and any material that isn't fully composted is returned to the bed."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "ACER's Role in the Community"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "What really stood out to me, though, was how the campus is using this process to help people. They don't just run the composting units themselves they're training others to do it too."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The community engagement team at the campus regularly holds hands-on workshops and demonstrations, especially for women and smallholder farmers. These sessions cover everything from setting up composting beds and choosing the right worms, to managing moisture and harvesting the compost. It's practical, low-cost, and something almost anyone can do with a bit of space and the right materials."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "By promoting vermicomposting, the university is helping communities create their own supply of organic fertilizer. This not only improves soil and crop health but also reduces the need to buy chemical fertilizers, which are often expensive and harmful to people and the environment. It's a small step, but it over time it results in stronger crops, healthier soil, better food, and more income stability."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "A Circular Way of Living",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "What I saw at the university's demo plots was more than just composting. It was a vision of how we can live more in tune with nature. Nothing is wasted. What one living thing discards, another transforms into life again."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "As I left the unit, I found myself thinking differently about the food scraps we throw away, the waste that piles up in cities and villages alike, and how often we overlook the simple, slow work of nature. Worms don't get much credit, but actually they deserve huge credit for their work."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "They're not just cleaning up our mess they're showing us a better way to grow."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
